---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { appStoreUrl } from '../../../lib/site';

export function getStaticPaths() {
  const features = [
    {
      slug: 'inkline-radio',
      shot: 'radio', shotAlt: 'The Inkline Radio player: a two-story morning bulletin with its running order',
      name: 'Inkline Radio',
      answer: 'Inkline Radio turns your briefing into a short spoken bulletin, read in a natural British voice, generated entirely on your phone. Play it over coffee or in the car; nothing you follow is sent anywhere to make it.',
      body: ['The script is written from the same on-device brief that appears in Today, then read by a neural voice that ships inside the app itself. There is no streaming service behind it and no per-listen cost, which is also why it works offline once the morning file is in.','Reading any single article aloud stays free for every reader, as an accessibility feature. The full spoken briefing across your day is part of Inkline Pro.'],
    },
    {
      slug: 'keywords',
      shot: 'watch', shotAlt: 'The Watch tab: journalists you follow and the keywords you track, in one place',
      name: 'Keywords',
      answer: 'A keyword flags stories instead of hiding them: clients, competitors, spokespeople. When a word you track appears in an arriving story, the story is marked, gathered in the Watch tab, and can notify you. Each keyword can also search the whole web, beyond your publications.',
      body: ['Mute rules make noise disappear. Keywords do the opposite job: they answer a question you set. The Watch tab collects every catch in one place, and the count shown is how many stories the term caught, a fact about the term rather than a badge nagging you to clear it.','Matching happens once, when a story arrives, and the result is stamped on the story itself. Rename or delete the rule later and the record of what was true at the time survives. Keywords are part of Inkline Pro, alongside monitors.'],
    },
    {
      slug: 'topic-monitoring',
      shot: 'feed', shotAlt: 'The Front Page with Watching and Trending rows above the lead story',
      name: 'Topic monitoring',
      answer: 'Inkline monitors any topic across the whole web, not just your subscriptions, and alerts you when new coverage appears. Type a company, a name or an issue once, and every fresh story lands in your feed with the rest of your morning.',
      body: ['Monitoring is the difference between a reader and a tool you can do the job with. A feed list shows you what your publications published; a monitor shows you what the web published about the things you answer for. Inkline runs both in the same place, so a client mention from a title you have never heard of arrives beside the front pages you check every day.','Alerts are optional per topic and arrive as notifications. Everything is processed for you on the device: there is no server reading your monitor list, which is also why it never appears in anyone else\u2019s analytics.'],
    },
    {
      slug: 'daily-briefs', name: 'Apple Intelligence daily briefs',
      shot: 'today', shotAlt: 'The Today page: Inkline Radio briefing card ready to play above the morning’s lead stories',
      answer: 'Inkline writes a daily brief from your own feeds using Apple Intelligence, entirely on the device. Nothing you read, follow or annotate is uploaded anywhere.',
      body: [
        'Every monitoring professional runs the same morning routine: scan everything, keep the six stories that matter, brief someone before nine. Inkline automates the first two steps. Overnight it reads what your feeds published, and by morning a brief is waiting on the Front Page, grouped by the interests you set during onboarding.',
        'Because the model runs on the device, the brief works on a plane and costs the same at any usage: nothing. It also means Inkline never sees your reading list, which for anyone monitoring sensitive client matters is not a nicety but a requirement.',
      ],
    },
    {
      slug: 'verified-directory', name: 'The verified directory',
      answer: 'Inkline ships a registry of 817 publications across 55 markets and 34 languages, hand-picked against a written rubric and feed-verified before every release. Access status is recorded for every title, and sources that fail verification drop out.',
      body: [
        'Feed directories rot. Publishers move endpoints, retire desks and redesign sites without announcing it, and the free directories that dominate search results largely stopped being maintained years ago. Inkline treats the directory as the product: every source is checked on a quarterly pass, and every publication page on this site carries the date of its last check.',
        'The directory also maps sections that are not publicly listed, and holds workarounds for publications that have withdrawn public feeds altogether. That work lives inside the app, which is why this site publishes section names and never endpoints.',
      ],
    },
    {
      slug: 'coverage-timeline', name: 'The coverage timeline',
      shot: 'coverage', shotAlt: 'The Coverage sheet: a framing read and the trajectory of one story across seven publications',
      answer: 'For any story, Inkline shows which other publications covered it and lines the headlines up side by side, so you can see the framing spread rather than a single telling.',
      body: [
        'Coverage analysis starts with a deceptively hard question: who else ran this? Inkline answers it inline. Under a story, "the coverage timeline" lists the other publications in your feeds that published on the same subject, with their headlines, so the difference in framing is visible at a glance.',
        'In practice this is how a monitor tells a wire ripple from a story with legs. One publication is a report; five publications with hardening headlines is a situation.',
      ],
    },
    {
      slug: 'offline-reading', name: 'Offline reading',
      shot: 'reader', shotAlt: 'The reader: full article text with bookmark, note, listen and type controls',
      answer: 'Inkline caches full articles on the device, so everything you follow is readable without a connection: on the Underground, in the air, or anywhere signal dies.',
      body: [
        'A reader that needs a connection is a reader that fails on the commute, which is exactly when most people do their reading. Inkline syncs while you have signal and keeps the full article text locally.',
        'Offline is also a side effect of the architecture. Because there is no server dependency, the app degrades gracefully: no spinner waiting on someone else\u2019s datacentre, just the last synced state of your world.',
      ],
    },
    {
      slug: 'ipad-and-pencil', name: 'iPad and Apple Pencil',
      answer: 'On iPad, Inkline becomes a landscape reading workspace with a Today front page, keyboard shortcuts, pointer support and handwritten Apple Pencil notes attached to articles.',
      body: [
        'The iPad version is not a stretched phone app. It has its own Today front page, a landscape layout built for reading at desk distance, and desktop-class input: keyboard shortcuts, pointer, drag and drop.',
        'The Pencil is the distinctive part. Notes are handwritten in the margin of the article they belong to, the way people actually annotate briefing packs, and they stay attached to the story for later retrieval.',
      ],
    },
    {
      slug: 'voices', name: 'Voices',
      shot: 'search', shotAlt: 'Search: Voices at the top, with publication results and access labels below',
      answer: 'Voices is Inkline\u2019s newsroom directory: follow individual journalists and desks rather than whole mastheads, and see who actually writes the coverage you monitor.',
      body: [
        'Media monitoring is ultimately about people. The byline that matters to your sector is a specific correspondent, not a publication, and Voices makes journalists first-class citizens: browse a newsroom, follow the bylines you care about, and read their work across everything they write.',
        'For comms professionals this doubles as list-building. Knowing who covers your patch, and how often, is the groundwork of every pitch, and Voices keeps that picture current as people move desks.',
      ],
    },
  ];
  return features.map((f) => ({ params: { feature: f.slug }, props: { f } }));
}
const { f } = Astro.props;
const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' }, { name: 'The app', url: '/app/' },
  { name: f.name, url: `/app/features/${f.slug}/` },
];
---
<Base
  title={`${f.name}: Inkline for iPhone and iPad | Inkline`}
  description={f.answer}
  path={`/app/features/${f.slug}/`}
  crumbs={crumbs}
>
  <div slot="masthead">
  <p class="kicker">Feature</p>
  <h1>{f.name}</h1>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p style="font-size:1.12rem">{f.answer}</p>

  {f.shot && (
    <figure class="shot">
      <img src={`/screens/${f.shot}.webp`} alt={f.shotAlt} width="720" height="1564" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
    </figure>
  )}
    {f.body.map((para) => <p>{para}</p>)}
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:2rem"><a class="btn" href={appStoreUrl(`web-feature-${f.slug}`)}>Open in Inkline</a></p>
  <p class="small">Screenshot of this feature to be placed here from the §11.1 asset handover; every image on this site is a real Inkline screenshot.</p>
</Base>

<style>
  .shot { margin: 2.5rem auto; max-width: 340px; }
  .shot img { width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 24px; border: 1px solid var(--rule); box-shadow: 0 24px 60px rgba(19, 31, 59, 0.18); }
</style>